Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:10:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rockchip: fix RGMII delays on the EAIDK-610

The EAIDK-610 generates the RGMII delays on the PHY side: the board vendor
device tree uses phy-mode = "rgmii-id" and leaves the MAC-side delay
properties unset.  The upstream description instead combines
phy-mode = "rgmii" with explicit tx_delay/rx_delay values, which applies the
delays twice.

With both delays in place the RTL8211E never establishes a link: the stmmac
driver probes, eth0 shows up, but the carrier stays down, so DHCP never
completes and the board has no network access.

Use phy-mode = "rgmii-id" and drop the MAC-side delays, matching the vendor
description.  Verified on hardware: the link comes up, eth0 obtains a DHCP
lease and traffic passes.

Fixes: 904f983256fd ("arm64: dts: rockchip: Add dts for a rk3399 based board EAIDK-610")

---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-eaidk-610.dts | 4 +---
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-eaidk-610.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-eaidk-610.dts
index b90bf26..5f2d6f2 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-eaidk-610.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3399-eaidk-610.dts
@@ -276,14 +276,12 @@
 	assigned-clock-parents = <&clkin_gmac>;
 	clock_in_out = "input";
 	phy-supply = <&vcc_lan>;
-	phy-mode = "rgmii";
+	phy-mode = "rgmii-id";
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&rgmii_pins>;
 	snps,reset-gpio = <&gpio3 RK_PB7 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
 	snps,reset-active-low;
 	snps,reset-delays-us = <0 10000 50000>;
-	tx_delay = <0x28>;
-	rx_delay = <0x11>;
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
